on this episode of snapchat spotify  engineer matt brown tells us how  kubernetes helps power the teams that  power Spotify increased deployments and  higher productivity are just the start  [Music]  thanks for trying to be Matt so how does  Spotify work when you use Spotify in  your laptop your mobile phone speaker in  your house if your app is connected to a  layer of services that we call the  perimeter series of gateways load  balancers things like that and then from  there depending on what you're doing in  the app you're connected to one of  hundreds or probably bout a thousand  different micro services that are  developed we used to run these all in  our own datacenter but over the years  we've moved fully to Google Cloud and  those micro services which are all  talking to each other are developed by  about 200 different teams within Spotify  and they're deploying those building  those independently based on one team  might work on a search feature the  playlist feature just different features  like that and then we have another group  of teams of infrastructure teams where  we're trying to build tools for those  teams to move faster do things in a  consistent way things like that so how  do you help those many many engineering  teams be able to focus on I'm going to  build this feature and then ship it  whether to staging or production and not  have to worry about all the  infrastructure so we do a lot as an  infrastructure team to try to make it  very simple to stand up a new service a  new feature there is a framework that  you can use basically gives you all the  basics of building a micro service  handling RPC calls logging connecting to  service discovery to find the other  services you need to talk to we also  make it very simple there's a single  button you can press in our developer  console to just create a new project new  source code repo and you get all that  stuff mentioned that I mentioned before  basically for free  you know just quote it from a template  so you're never really starting from  scratch and then you have to manage all  of these and figure out how they're  gonna actually stay alive or scale you  built your own system to do all that yes  we did so we started to adopt docker  it's inners very early on I think before  it was even version 1.0 and we were  really excited by it because it added a  bunch of new things that we didn't  really have great answers for a Spotify  at the time it used to be very painful  for teams to deploy changes to their  services  they'd frequently fail rolling back from  a failed deployment was pretty tricky  and as a result people teams just would  not deploy very often because there was  a lot of risk in payment that so we were  very interested in soccer because we  thought that building containers would  make it a lot simpler we could just have  deployments would be less a blur and if  you just want to roll back because each  container is kind of atomic totally  separated that it would be very simple  another big benefit is that it was just  a lot easier for elders to run the  service themselves on their laptop even  if it didn't really look like production  so the main reason we were super  interested in soccer to start with  wishes to make deploying easier making  running services easier and then to  increase the rate at which people were  deploying changes and just being  productive basically and is there  anything looking back on that at least  the first chunk of that transition that  you wish you did differently I would say  one thing that I wish we did a little  bit differently we were very conscious  as an infrastructure team that we're  changing out are basically the platform  that we were on applications on in  production which was working pretty fun  before it has some features that we wish  we had we they were missing that we wish  we had but if things weren't on fire or  anything like that so we wanted to be  very sure that what we move people to is  super stable they don't really notice  any problems and it's just all positives  for them to move I think as a part of  that we probably were a little hesitant  to take risks and we didn't want really  any team to move their service fully to  kubernetes until we were as a ever  stronger team a hundred and ten percent  prepare for that in retrospect I think  we could have accepted some more risk  the team the engineers on all of our  teams building these services they know  better than we do as an infrastructure  which services are super important which  services are internal facing and not  important at all and stance of downtime  so I think we could have let them take  some more risk and tell us what would be  you know like do some more experiments  themselves and we probably just learn a  few things a little bit faster are there  other things that you wish you did more  of as you're going through this  transition process I think one thing  that we found that kind of surprised us  as we did this is we didn't have to do  as much selling or evangelizing of the  rosy-cheeked aid to the engineering team  is that Spotify part of that's because  they just they dirty knew about  kubernetes again obviously is talked  about quite a lot in the development  community but also we found that some  early adopting teams were really happy  with the results and so they would go  out and talk to other teams and give  talks of like an internal conference  that we do or just they would try to  motivate their peers so I wish one thing  we could have done more of perhaps has  tried to take advantage of that create  more sort of champions internally  because I was rare of really those  engineers who did that work were really  the best selling point that we had for  convincing other teams to spend time  with us if you enjoyed this episode  check out the stack chat playlist for  more videos  you

